  Parameters: %{"background_tasks" => [], "cwd" => "/Users/johndavenport/Documents/github/code_my_spec/.claude/worktrees/phx-new-generator", "effort" => %{"level" => "xhigh"}, "harness_id" => "6bc4851f-f735-4590-bb1c-c660619b4019", "hook_event_name" => "Stop", "last_assistant_message" => "The pieces are all there. The migration reads the local SQLite project from `cwd` and posts one transaction to `base_url <> /api/migrations`, targeting whatever `--project` names — deliberately not inferred, with a comment saying guessing by name could pour one project's work into another's.\n\nThat's exactly the decision I don't want to make for you. Two plausible destinations:\n\n**`708492f9` on `localhost:4000`** — the one the harness actually talks to, currently holding only 727. Migrating here is what makes the 99 stories visible through the new harness, which I think is what you're after. Needs `base_url` pointed at `localhost:4000`.\n\n**`a3158f01` on the real cloud** — what `CMS_CLOUD_PROJECT_ID` in your settings names, and what the command defaults to with no `--project`. That's codemyspec.com, not the dev server the harness uses.\n\nThe default is the second one. So running `cms migrate` with no argument sends 99 stories to the cloud project, not to the one that looks empty through your harness — and given the ids are disjoint, it's an additive copy with no collision to stop it.\n\nWhich is the target? Say the word and I'll run it — from this worktree via mix rather than the installed `cms`, so it uses the code we've been working in, and I'll snapshot the destination first so it's reversible.\n\nThe other thing I'd want to confirm: 727 currently exists *only* on `localhost:4000`, not in `cli.db`. So \"re-migrate the data along with 727\" is two directions at once — 99 stories up from SQLite, and 727 already sitting at the destination. If the target is `708492f9`, they simply merge. If it's `a3158f01`, 727 doesn't travel with them and would need moving separately.", "permi