  Parameters: %{"cwd" => "/Users/johndavenport/Documents/github/code_my_spec/.claude/worktrees/phx-new-generator", "effort" => %{"level" => "xhigh"}, "harness_id" => "6bc4851f-f735-4590-bb1c-c660619b4019", "hook_event_name" => "PreToolUse", "permission_mode" => "default", "prompt_id" => "f77d6934-4e5d-4216-954b-2e831949cd8a", "session_id" => "80dcd471-007d-4438-8f86-f511c41e6e37", "tool_input" => %{"command" => "git commit -q -m \"$(cat <<'EOF'\nStop two CLI migrations from taking the whole CLI down\n\nBoth used `modify`, which compiles to ALTER COLUMN — a statement SQLite\ndoes not have. And because the migrator is a supervised child, that is\nnot a failed migration: it is an application that will not start. Every\nCLI on a database older than 20260801 was wedged, which is how a\nmigration run surfaced it rather than anyone's tests.\n\nkey_files_on_harness_and_path (mine) keeps the part that matters — the\nunique index moving to (project_id, harness_id, path), which is what\nconflict_target names — and drops the NOT NULL and the foreign key\nchange. Reproducing those on SQLite means rebuilding the table, and the\nguarantee they buy is already covered here: the orphan delete removes the\nrows that could collide, and every writer names its working copy. The\nconsequence is stated in the file — on SQLite that constraint is the\napplication's, not the database's.\n\nenvironments_timestamps_to_microseconds becomes an empty migration.\nSQLite is dynamically typed, so datetime precision comes from how Ecto\ncasts, which is schema-side and shared. Kept rather than deleted so the\nversion numbers stay aligned across the two sets.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01JPoSfcCJqSqG8iGY9nfyfL\nEOF\n)\" && git log --oneline -1", "description" => "Commit the CLI migration fixes"}, "tool_name" => "Bash", "tool_use_id" => "toolu_013meAzndynUbuNWQhaeUeRr", "transcript_path" => "/Users/johndavenport/.c